WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.948
A community nutrition program is not defined
by the information it distributes.

2
00:00:04.948 --> 00:00:11.479
It is defined by a nutrition need, a community
context, a plausible change strategy, and a

3
00:00:11.479 --> 00:00:12.295
decision process.

4
00:00:12.295 --> 00:00:15.089
Begin by asking who needs to decide what.

5
00:00:15.089 --> 00:00:19.298
A health department may need to choose which
neighborhood to serve.

6
00:00:19.298 --> 00:00:22.512
A school partnership may need to revise
delivery.

7
00:00:22.512 --> 00:00:26.224
A funder may need evidence of reach and
benefit.

8
00:00:26.224 --> 00:00:32.142
That decision determines which community
voices, data, activities, and outcomes matter.

9
00:00:32.142 --> 00:00:38.133
In this lesson, we will move from assessment
to a logic model, then from measurement to

10
00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:38.507
action.

11
00:00:38.507 --> 00:00:41.543
The result is not a fictional perfect program.

12
00:00:41.543 --> 00:00:47.110
It is a bounded design that makes assumptions
visible, treats community members as partners,

13
00:00:47.110 --> 00:00:50.688
and can be improved when evidence contradicts
the plan.

14
00:00:50.834 --> 00:00:53.819
"The community" must be operationally defined.

15
00:00:53.819 --> 00:01:00.928
It may be a geographic area, a school network,
a cultural group, a service population, or

16
00:01:00.928 --> 00:01:01.817
overlapping communities.

17
00:01:01.817 --> 00:01:04.929
State the boundary and who may be excluded by
it.

18
00:01:04.929 --> 00:01:10.710
Then assemble a comprehensive picture: health
outcomes, dietary patterns, food access,

19
00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:15.439
economic and environmental conditions,
existing services, community assets, and

20
00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:18.067
experiences that explain the numbers.

21
00:01:18.067 --> 00:01:23.130
Use multiple sources because no single dataset
represents the whole system.

22
00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:26.153
Administrative data can show service use.

23
00:01:26.153 --> 00:01:28.755
Surveys can estimate behaviors.

24
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:34.419
Interviews and listening sessions can reveal
barriers, trust, and priorities.

25
00:01:34.419 --> 00:01:37.148
Asset mapping shows what already works.

26
00:01:37.148 --> 00:01:40.337
Assessment is not a hunt for deficits.

27
00:01:40.337 --> 00:01:46.335
It is a shared interpretation of needs,
strengths, resources, and constraints that can

28
00:01:46.335 --> 00:01:48.181
support a legitimate priority.

29
00:01:48.338 --> 00:01:53.249
Engagement is not one feedback meeting after
the program has been designed.

30
00:01:53.249 --> 00:01:58.621
Identify residents affected by the issue,
organizations already doing related work,

31
00:01:58.621 --> 00:02:03.504
implementers, funders, data stewards, and
people who can remove barriers.

32
00:02:03.504 --> 00:02:07.242
Clarify which decisions each partner can
influence.

33
00:02:07.242 --> 00:02:13.173
Compensate participation when possible and
provide information in accessible forms.

34
00:02:13.173 --> 00:02:18.416
Ask whose interpretation is missing, who may
be burdened by data collection, and whose

35
00:02:18.416 --> 00:02:21.412
trust is required for the work to continue.

36
00:02:21.412 --> 00:02:24.512
Then establish a transparent priority rule.

37
00:02:24.512 --> 00:02:31.136
A team might compare magnitude, disparity,
urgency, feasibility, existing assets,

38
00:02:31.136 --> 00:02:34.448
community concern, and potential benefit.

39
00:02:34.448 --> 00:02:38.071
The weights are value judgments, so they
should be visible.

40
00:02:38.071 --> 00:02:44.327
Collaborative priority setting improves both
relevance and the chances that a program will

41
00:02:44.327 --> 00:02:45.290
be used.

42
00:02:45.410 --> 00:02:49.798
Turn a broad concern into a statement that can
guide action.

43
00:02:49.798 --> 00:02:55.911
Name the population, nutrition-related
condition or behavior, place, time period,

44
00:02:55.911 --> 00:02:57.745
comparison, and evidence.

45
00:02:57.745 --> 00:03:00.602
Separate observation from explanation.

46
00:03:00.602 --> 00:03:07.796
"Fruit and vegetable intake is low" is
incomplete without the instrument, threshold,

47
00:03:07.796 --> 00:03:09.594
population, and uncertainty.

48
00:03:09.594 --> 00:03:14.403
"Access causes the behavior" is a hypothesis
until supported.

49
00:03:14.403 --> 00:03:19.715
Add the community assets that could change the
condition and the structural constraints that

50
00:03:19.715 --> 00:03:21.992
a nutrition class alone cannot solve.

51
00:03:21.992 --> 00:03:24.977
Avoid defining people as the problem.

52
00:03:24.977 --> 00:03:30.546
The statement should locate a modifiable part
of a system while recognizing conditions

53
00:03:30.546 --> 00:03:32.260
outside the program's control.

54
00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:37.394
Finish with a decision question, such as
whether a particular service strategy can

55
00:03:37.394 --> 00:03:42.133
reach the intended group and improve a
measured intermediate outcome within one

56
00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:42.923
program cycle.

57
00:03:43.058 --> 00:03:47.191
A logic model makes the program's causal story
reviewable.

58
00:03:47.191 --> 00:03:53.530
Inputs are resources such as staff, partner
time, space, food, data, and funding.

59
00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:55.763
Activities are what the program does.

60
00:03:55.763 --> 00:04:01.142
Outputs are direct products: sessions
delivered, people reached, referrals

61
00:04:01.142 --> 00:04:04.130
completed, or environmental changes adopted.

62
00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:07.485
Outcomes are changes expected after those
outputs.

63
00:04:07.485 --> 00:04:13.792
Short-term outcomes can include knowledge,
confidence, goals, readiness, or partner

64
00:04:13.792 --> 00:04:14.423
capacity.

65
00:04:14.423 --> 00:04:18.658
Medium-term outcomes can include behavior or
setting changes.

66
00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:22.931
Long-term outcomes concern maintenance and
population results.

67
00:04:22.931 --> 00:04:28.862
Draw arrows only when the team can state why
one element should influence the next.

68
00:04:28.862 --> 00:04:33.747
Beneath each arrow, record assumptions and
external factors.

69
00:04:33.747 --> 00:04:36.120
A logic model is not proof.

70
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:41.448
It is a compact set of hypotheses that tells
the evaluator what evidence would strengthen

71
00:04:41.448 --> 00:04:43.224
or weaken the program theory.

72
00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:47.452
Match the intervention to the cause the
assessment identifies.

73
00:04:47.452 --> 00:04:55.106
A knowledge barrier may justify education, but
education alone cannot change store inventory,

74
00:04:55.106 --> 00:04:58.638
transportation, prices, scheduling, or
organizational policy.

75
00:04:58.638 --> 00:05:02.924
Individual-level strategies can build skills
and confidence.

76
00:05:02.924 --> 00:05:08.087
Setting-level strategies can change
availability, placement, procurement, or

77
00:05:08.087 --> 00:05:09.378
service routines.

78
00:05:09.378 --> 00:05:15.674
Partnership and sector strategies can
coordinate resources or policies across

79
00:05:15.674 --> 00:05:16.304
organizations.

80
00:05:16.304 --> 00:05:21.228
A multilevel program should explain how the
parts reinforce one another.

81
00:05:21.228 --> 00:05:27.139
Define the dose, reach, delivery setting,
implementer, adaptation rules, and fidelity

82
00:05:27.139 --> 00:05:29.289
elements that must remain.

83
00:05:29.289 --> 00:05:32.656
Also name what the program does not claim to
change.

84
00:05:32.656 --> 00:05:38.664
A well-bounded strategy directs resources
toward a modifiable mechanism instead of

85
00:05:38.664 --> 00:05:44.672
asking participants to overcome a structural
barrier the program leaves untouched.

86
00:05:44.810 --> 00:05:49.326
Choose measures from the evaluation question,
not from convenience alone.

87
00:05:49.326 --> 00:05:54.844
A 24-hour recall, food record, food-frequency
questionnaire, and brief screener capture

88
00:05:54.844 --> 00:05:58.356
different time frames and impose different
burdens.

89
00:05:58.356 --> 00:06:05.033
Self-report is affected by memory, reactivity,
day-to-day variation, and systematic reporting

90
00:06:05.033 --> 00:06:05.640
bias.

91
00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:12.126
Repeating a measure can reduce some random
variability, but repetition does not

92
00:06:12.126 --> 00:06:14.288
automatically remove systematic bias.

93
00:06:14.288 --> 00:06:20.035
Administrative records, direct observation,
environmental audits, or biomarkers may answer

94
00:06:20.035 --> 00:06:23.484
different questions and introduce different
constraints.

95
00:06:23.484 --> 00:06:29.219
Use a validated instrument for the intended
population when available, pilot the process,

96
00:06:29.219 --> 00:06:30.984
and document missing data.

97
00:06:30.984 --> 00:06:37.285
State whether the outcome concerns an
individual, a group mean, change over time,

98
00:06:37.285 --> 00:06:38.739
reach, or implementation.

99
00:06:38.739 --> 00:06:44.001
Measurement is part of the program design
because weak evidence can produce a confident

100
00:06:44.001 --> 00:06:45.129
but wrong decision.

101
00:06:45.266 --> 00:06:49.119
An evaluation cannot answer every interesting
question.

102
00:06:49.119 --> 00:06:51.695
Prioritize those that partners can use.

103
00:06:51.695 --> 00:06:57.202
Separate implementation questions—who was
reached, what was delivered, and with what

104
00:06:57.202 --> 00:06:59.705
fidelity—from outcome questions about change.

105
00:06:59.705 --> 00:07:05.859
Specify the indicator, data source, collection
time, comparison, and analysis before looking

106
00:07:05.859 --> 00:07:07.397
at the result.

107
00:07:07.397 --> 00:07:13.036
A pre-post change may be useful for program
improvement but does not by itself establish

108
00:07:13.036 --> 00:07:15.292
that the program caused the change.

109
00:07:15.292 --> 00:07:21.519
A comparison group can strengthen a causal
claim, but only when selection, timing,

110
00:07:21.519 --> 00:07:23.914
contamination, and ethics are addressed.

111
00:07:23.914 --> 00:07:29.272
Qualitative evidence can explain why reach or
outcomes differ across groups.

112
00:07:29.272 --> 00:07:33.009
Document protocol changes and uncertainty.

113
00:07:33.009 --> 00:07:38.943
Credibility comes from a design proportionate
to the decision, not from using the most

114
00:07:38.943 --> 00:07:40.215
complicated method available.

115
00:07:40.346 --> 00:07:46.064
Consider a hypothetical neighborhood where
partners identify cost, transportation, and

116
00:07:46.064 --> 00:07:50.066
unfamiliar preparation as barriers to produce
use.

117
00:07:50.066 --> 00:07:56.431
The assets include a trusted community center,
a mobile market, and resident educators.

118
00:07:56.431 --> 00:08:02.043
The pilot combines scheduled produce access
with optional preparation demonstrations and

119
00:08:02.043 --> 00:08:03.063
referral support.

120
00:08:03.063 --> 00:08:08.897
Outputs include delivery reliability,
households reached, repeat participation, and

121
00:08:08.897 --> 00:08:10.193
referrals completed.

122
00:08:10.193 --> 00:08:13.561
Short-term outcomes include confidence and
intent.

123
00:08:13.561 --> 00:08:19.673
Medium-term outcomes may include reported
purchasing or preparation behavior and

124
00:08:19.673 --> 00:08:20.895
organizational adoption.

125
00:08:20.895 --> 00:08:26.865
The team also tracks price, missed stops,
participant burden, and differences in reach

126
00:08:26.865 --> 00:08:27.783
across subgroups.

127
00:08:27.783 --> 00:08:31.125
This example does not prove the strategy
works.

128
00:08:31.125 --> 00:08:36.692
It shows how assessment evidence, partner
roles, a logic model, measures, and decision

129
00:08:36.692 --> 00:08:39.262
rules can be connected before launch.

130
00:08:39.262 --> 00:08:44.556
The final question is whether evidence
supports expansion, revision, or stopping.

131
00:08:44.714 --> 00:08:47.419
Complete the design with a decision record.

132
00:08:47.419 --> 00:08:50.965
What context and community boundary are in
scope?

133
00:08:50.965 --> 00:08:52.406
Who shares authority?

134
00:08:52.406 --> 00:08:55.697
Which assessed priority was selected, and by
what rule?

135
00:08:55.697 --> 00:08:59.179
What change mechanism does the logic model
propose?

136
00:08:59.179 --> 00:09:02.138
Which activities and outputs operationalize
it?

137
00:09:02.138 --> 00:09:05.046
What outcomes and measures match the decision?

138
00:09:05.046 --> 00:09:08.720
What evidence and comparison support the
conclusion?

139
00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:13.209
How did reach, burden, and results differ
across groups?

140
00:09:13.209 --> 00:09:16.462
Which limitations could change the
interpretation?

141
00:09:16.462 --> 00:09:23.364
Finally, who will act, by when, and what
result will trigger continuation, revision,

142
00:09:23.364 --> 00:09:24.957
scale, or stop?

143
00:09:24.957 --> 00:09:31.475
Share findings in forms each audience can use,
including negative or uncertain results.

144
00:09:31.475 --> 00:09:37.062
Community nutrition improves when evaluation
is not an after-action report, but a

145
00:09:37.062 --> 00:09:41.717
transparent learning system embedded from
assessment through the next decision.
